 # Slow trend survives where most crypto edges fail
 
-Out of more than a decade of research, only one family of crypto timing rules holds up after costs and out-of-sample testing at a level a $1,000 long-only Coinbase spot trader can use: **slow trend following plus volatility scaling on BTC (and ETH)**. Even that family mainly **cuts drawdowns and raises risk-adjusted return. It does not reliably beat buy-and-hold on raw return**, and the BTC-specific out-of-sample evidence is split. Most other documented anomalies fall into one of four groups. Some are real but not available to this trader: carry, the variance risk premium, arbitrage, order-flow and MEV edges. Some are real gross but die under retail fees, microcap exclusion or the no-shorting rule: cross-sectional momentum, size, short-horizon reversal, overnight-session rules. Some are front-loaded or decayed: listing pops, Wikipedia and Twitter attention, calendar effects. The rest rest on too few observations to test, such as halving cycles and MVRV tops. The hurdle that decides most of these verdicts is cost. At the user's observed Coinbase tier of **0.25% maker / 0.50% taker per side**, a round trip costs 0.50–1.00% before spread. That is 3–7 times what most academic studies assume, so anything that trades weekly or faster starts tens of percentage points per year behind. The practical recipe has three parts. Build a BTC/ETH-dominant core on a slow, volatility-scaled trend rule. Add avoidance filters drawn from the evidence on decay, survivorship and events. Treat derivatives, on-chain and sentiment data as at most crash-risk overlays, since much of their content turns out to be repackaged price momentum. Validate every addition against the existing candidate, not against BTC, with trial counting and an untouched post-ETF holdout.
