 The literature changes the question from "which edge beats Bitcoin" to "which rule keeps one in Bitcoin with less pain, at a cost one can afford." The evidence answers the second question far more convincingly than the first. Crypto's documented alpha has largely migrated to people this account cannot be: short sellers, leveraged carry traders, option sellers, colocated market makers, MEV searchers and insiders. What remains for long-only retail is a well-sized trend exposure and the discipline to avoid known losers. That remainder is still worth having. BIS found most crypto-app users lost money on BTC, largely by buying after rallies ([BIS Bulletin 69](https://www.bis.org/publ/bisbull69.htm)), and a pre-committed rule guards against that behaviour even when it adds no alpha.
 
-For the user's project, the implication is to stop searching for a second return engine and to treat further work as risk engineering around the incumbent. The unresolved question that matters most is not another signal. It is the fee tier: at 0.50/0.90 instead of 0.25/0.50, the margin that separates a slow trend rule from break-even shrinks noticeably. Confirming the tier in the Coinbase app, and re-running the incumbent at both rates on the untouched 2024–2026 holdout, is worth more than any overlay in the ranking above.
